e","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"): the connected components of ","type":"text"},{"code":"lineGraph(s:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", as positions, without building","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"it. Singletons are kept (HyperNetX drops them by default), an empty hyperedge is its own","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"component, and components are ordered by their least position, each ascending. The vertex","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"s-components are ","type":"text"},{"code":"dual().sConnectedComponents(s:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph":{"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"DirectedHypergraph","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics"}]},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop (XGI’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"DiHypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" allows both).","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","role":"symbol","url":"\/document